##
    Telling the compiler to stop saying something.

    A diagnostic carries one of three severities. An error means the meaning is
    genuinely unpredictable, and it stops compilation. A warning means the meaning is clear and
    probably not what was intended. An opinion means the meaning is clear, intended and
    correct, and the language would still write it differently.

    Only the last two can be silenced. An error never can — the whole point of a mechanism that
    quiets the compiler is that it must not be able to make it lie. And nothing here stops a
    build: getting one of these lines wrong is itself only a warning, because a feature reached
    for to hear less would be worse than useless if a typo in it were fatal.

    This program compiles with nothing said about it. Delete any of the 'ignore' lines and the
    thing below it has something to say.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Console.WriteLine("== silencing one line ==");

        ##
            'Console.WriteLine("")' writes an empty line, and so does 'Console.WriteLine()'
            with nothing at all. Writing the empty string says a thing the call already does,
            which is PC0340, an opinion.

            The directive covers the next line carrying code, so it may sit above the line it
            is about rather than jammed against it. A blank line between them changes nothing.
        ##
        # ignore opinion
        Console.WriteLine("");

        ##
            Naming the identifier instead says which one is expected. It is the narrower of the
            two, and the one to reach for where a line might grow a second diagnostic later
            that should still be heard.
        ##
        # ignore PC0340
        Console.WriteLine("");

        ##
            Words after the target are prose, so a directive can say why it is there. This is
            the form worth writing: a reader who meets it a year later learns the reason
            without having to guess it.
        ##
        # ignore opinion deliberate, to show the blank line above the total
        Console.WriteLine("");

        Console.WriteLine("== and prose stays prose ==");

        ##
            A comment beginning with the word 'ignore' is a remark, not a directive. Nothing is
            recognized until 'warning', 'opinion', or something shaped like an identifier
            follows, so an ordinary sentence is read the way it was written.
        ##
        # ignore the fractions for now, they come later
        Console.WriteLine("this line is not silenced, and has nothing to silence");

        Program.Counting();
    end function

    ##
        A range loop counts with integers, so writing 'integer' on its counter says a thing the
        loop already said. That is PC0111, and it is the same shape of remark as the empty
        string: nothing is wrong, and a word could go.

        Here it is kept, because the point of the section is that it can be — and the directive
        above it records the choice.
    ##
    shared function Counting()
        Console.WriteLine("== a counter that says its type ==");

        # ignore PC0111 kept deliberately, to show a directive inside a function
        loop for integer i = 1 to 3
            Console.WriteLine("  " + i);
        end loop
    end function

end model
